{\pard\sb360\sa120\keepn\b 1-journal/corpus/ \u183? what the evidence map is allowed to see\par}
{\pard\sa160 The corpus is the journal's own object, published in advance (appendix C.6: "the corpus is the journal's"). It has two parts, and the map states its coverage as exactly what was searched, never as "the literature".\par}
{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b The venues: {\f1 venues.yaml}\par}
{\pard\sa160 The journal lists on which the map searches, each journal with its ISSNs and the lists it belongs to: UT Dallas 24, Financial Times 50, and the Chartered ABS Academic Journal Guide grades 4* and 4. {\f1 search} says which lists are switched on; {\f1 hand_list} says whether the hand list below is also shown. {\f1 mark1 corpus} resolves every ISSN to an OpenAlex source once and caches it in {\f1 sources.json}; journals that do not resolve are named in every coverage statement.\par}
{\pard\sa160 For each paper, SIEVE's contribution module ({\f1 machine/retrieval.py}) searches those venues through OpenAlex:\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 1.\tab Keyword queries built from each contribution claim and the paper's title: the claim whole, the words the claim shares with the title, and those again against titles only, where a short old note can still win.\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 2.\tab The works most cited among the hits, and among what the paper itself cites, kept when they are in the venues. This is the step that reaches older antecedents, which often have no abstract in OpenAlex and rank low on keyword relevance.\par}
{\pard\sa160 Up to 60 candidates, with the queries and what found each one, are kept in the run's {\f1 sieve/candidates.json}, so a miss can be traced and the run replays offline. Candidates without an abstract are shown to the model by title, venue and year only; a placement that rests on one carries {\f1 basis: title_and_known_work} and the editor sees it. Membership of the three lists was compiled from public sources on the dates in the file and needs a coauthor's check before use on a real paper.\par}
{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b The hand list: {\f1 corpus.yaml}\par}
{\pard\sa160 A hand-named list of papers, enriched from OpenAlex into {\f1 openalex/<id>.json} (title, authors, year, venue, abstract, topics). It is what the Stage-1 demonstration ran on before the venues existed, and it remains useful for work the venues do not reach: a journal can name a book or a working paper here. Where OpenAlex has no abstract, the {\f1 summary} written in {\f1 corpus.yaml} is shown instead; those summaries were written by Claude Code for the demonstration and need a coauthor's check.\par}
{\pard\sa160 The enrichment also checks the list: a wrong DOI comes back as a different paper. One did, during the build, and was corrected.\par}
{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b OpenAlex access\par}
{\pard\sa160 OpenAlex gives every network a small free daily budget, shared by everyone behind the same address, and a free key with its own budget to anyone who registers an email. Set {\f1 MARK1_OPENALEX_KEY} to use a key, or {\f1 MARK1_OPENALEX_MAILTO} for the polite pool. Nothing in Mark 1 sends an address on its own.\par}
